# SITE_ADDRESS is the public host name, for example komplek.example.com. A bare host name makes
# Caddy obtain and renew a TLS certificate for it and redirect HTTP to HTTPS on its own; ORIGIN in
# .env must be the same address with https:// in front.
{$SITE_ADDRESS} {
	reverse_proxy app:3000 {
		# Resend a GET or HEAD whose round trip to the app failed, instead of answering the browser
		# 502 (#213, found in the caddy log as "msg":"EOF").
		#
		# The cause was in Bun 1.3.14: its node:http closed a kept-alive connection with no response
		# and no `Connection: close` when the next request arrived while the previous response was
		# still being finished (oven-sh/bun#31889, fixed in Bun 1.4.0). adapter-node served every
		# static file that way, so a request Caddy sent on a connection that had just carried one
		# could read EOF, and it never reached SvelteKit. Go's transport already resends such a GET
		# by itself when the browser came over HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2. It did not when the browser came
		# over HTTP/3, because Caddy then passes on a request body of unknown length that cannot be
		# rewound, so those reached the browser as a 502. The image has run Bun 1.4.2 since #223, so
		# the retries below now stay as a safety net rather than the fix.
		#
		# What the two lines below do in Caddy 2.11, per
		# https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/directives/reverse_proxy and LoadBalancing.tryAgain
		# in modules/caddyhttp/reverseproxy/reverseproxy.go of v2.11.4:
		# - Retries stay off until lb_retries or lb_try_duration is set. lb_retries 2 allows two more
		#   attempts after the first, sent straight away: lb_try_interval only defaults to 250ms when
		#   lb_try_duration is set.
		# - A request whose connection could not be opened at all is retried whatever its method,
		#   since the app never received it. Caddy always does that once retries are on.
		# - A request whose connection was opened but whose round trip then failed is retried only
		#   when it matches lb_retry_match. Without the block that means GET alone; the block adds
		#   HEAD. Both are safe methods (RFC 9110 section 9.2.1), and the one GET here known to change
		#   something, /verify?token=, lands on the same verified address when it arrives twice (see
		#   its load function). After the Bun drop above, the retry is the first attempt SvelteKit sees.
		# - POST, and every method other than GET and HEAD, is never resent once the app may have
		#   received it: a form action is not idempotent, and running one twice could record a
		#   payment twice. Such a request still answers 502 when the connection drops under it; the
		#   Bun upgrade in #223 removed that cause.
		lb_retries 2
		lb_retry_match {
			method GET HEAD
		}
	}
}
